e unique row with a hole in that column, i.e. $r_j=\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$.  If $r_j\\\\in\\\\{1,n\\\\}$ the column contains a single contiguous block of covered squares; otherwise it contains two non‑empty blocks:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{above block }A_j=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i< r_j\\\\},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\text{below block }B_j=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid r_j< i\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nConsider the set\\n\\\\[\\nS=\\\\{(r_j-1,j)\\\\mid r_j>1\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nFor each $j$ with $r_j>1$, the cell $(r_j-1,j)$ is the lowest cell of the above block $A_j$.  The cells of $S$ lie in distinct rows (they are $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$) and in distinct columns (the columns $j$ are all different).  Suppose a single rectangle $R$ contained two distinct cells $(r_j-1,j)$ and $(r_{j'}-1,j')$ with $j\\\\neq j'$.  Without loss of generality $r_j<r_{j'}$.  Then the row interval of $R$ contains the rows $r_j-1,\\\\dots,r_{j'}-1$, and the column interval contains $j$ and $j'$.  Consequently $R$ also contains the cell $(r_j,j)$, which is a hole – a contradiction.  Hence no rectangle can cover two different cells of $S$, and each cell of $S$ requires its own rectangle.  Since $|S|=n-1$, at least $n-1$ rectangles are necessary to cover all above blocks.\\n\\nAn analogous argument applied to the set\\n\\\\[\\nT=\\\\{(r_j+1,j)\\\\mid r_j<n\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\n(the topmost cells of the below blocks) shows that at least another $n-1$ rectangles are needed for the below blocks.  A rectangle covering a cell of $S$ cannot also cover a cell of $T$, because any such rectangle would have to contain the hole $(r_j,j)$ lying between the two cells.  Therefore the total number of rectangles required is at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n---\\n**2.  A construction using exactly $2n-2$ tiles.**\\n\\nChoose any permutation $\\\\pi$ (for instance the identity $\\\\pi(i)=i$).  For each column $j$ place:\\n- a vertical tile $V_j^{\\\\text{up}}$ covering the cells $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i< r_j$ (if $r_j>1$);\\n- a vertical tile $V_j^