\text{down}}$ covering the cells $(i,j)$ with $r_j< i\\\\le n$ (if $r_j<n$).\\nEach $V_j^{\\\\text{up}}$ and $V_j^{\\\\text{down}}$ is a $1\\\\times (r_j-1)$ or $1\\\\times (n-r_j)$ rectangle, respectively.  The two tiles in a given column are disjoint because the hole $(r_j,j)$ separates them, and tiles belonging to different columns are disjoint because they occupy different columns.  All squares except the $n$ holes are covered, and the total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{j=1}^n \\\\bigl(\\\\mathbf{1}_{\\\\{r_j>1\\\\}}+\\\\mathbf{1}_{\\\\{r_j<n\\\\}}\\\\bigr)=2n-2,\\n\\\\]\\nsince exactly one column has $r_j=1$ (the column of the hole in the first row) and exactly one column has $r_j=n$ (the column of the hole in the last row).  Thus $2n-2$ tiles always suffice.\\n---\\n**3.  Conclusion.**\\n\\nThe lower bound $2n-2$ and the explicit construction achieving $2n-2$ coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nHence Matilda cannot do better than $4048$ tiles, and she can achieve this number.\\n\\n### Final Answer ###\\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\"\n}"
