alisation of the uncovered squares\\n\\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*.  There exists a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $p(i)$.  Equivalently, for each column $j$ there is a unique row $r_j$ with $p(r_j)=j$; we denote this row by\\n\\\\[\\n    r_j:=p^{-1}(j)\\\\;.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the uncovered square in column $j$ is the cell $(r_j,j)$.\\n\\n#### 2.  Decomposition of the board\\n\\nFor a fixed column $j$ the uncovered square $(r_j,j)$ splits the column into two disjoint vertical segments:\\n\\\\[\\n    \\\\text{Top}(j)=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i< r_j\\\\},\\\\qquad\\n    \\\\text{Bottom}(j)=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid r_j< i\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll unit squares of the board belong either to a top segment, a bottom segment, or are uncovered.  Consequently the $n^2-n$ squares that must be tiled are exactly the union of all top segments and all bottom segments.\\n\\n#### 3.  A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles\\n\\nConsider the set $T:=\\\\{(r_j-1,j)\\\\mid r_j>1\\\\}$ of the cells that lie *directly* above each uncovered square (the cell immediately above $(r_j,j)$, when such a cell exists).  There are exactly $n-1$ such cells because precisely one column contains the uncovered square in the first row (for that column $r_j=1$, and no cell lies above it).\\n\\n**Lemma 1.**  No two cells of $T$ can belong to the same rectangular tile.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile $R$ contains two distinct cells $(r_j-1,j)$ and $(r_k-1,k)$ with $j\\\\neq k$.  Since $R$ is a rectangle, it also contains the four corner cells of the axis‑aligned rectangle spanned by these two cells, in particular the cell $(r_j-1,k)$.  Because $r_j-1<r_j$, the column $k$ has its uncovered square at row $r_k$.  If $r_k\\\\le r_j-1$, then $(r_j-1,k)$ lies on or above the uncovered square $(r_k,k)$, contradicting the fact that a tile cannot cover an uncovered square.  If $r_k> r_j-1$, then $(r_j-1,k)$ lies strictly between rows $1$ and $r_k-1$, i.e. it belongs to the top segment of column $k$, which is allowed.  However, the rectangle $R$ also contains the cell $(r_k-1,j)$.  By the same reasoning we obtain a contradiction with the uncovered square in column $j$.  Hence such a tile $R$ cannot exist.  ∎\\n\\nBecause the $n-1$ cells of $T$ must be covered and each tile can contain at most one of them, at least $n-1$ tiles are required to cover all top segments.\\n\\nAn analogous argument applied to the set\\n\\\\[\\n    B:=\\\\{(r_j+1,j)\\\\mid r_j<n\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nof the cells directly below each uncovered square (again $|B|=n-1$) shows that at least $n-1$ tiles are needed to cover all bottom segments.\\n\\nSince a tile covering a top cell cannot simultaneously cover a bottom cell (the uncovered square would lie inside the tile), the two families of tiles are disjoint.  Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least\\n\\\\[\\n    (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n#### 4.  A construction using exactly $2n-2$ tiles\\n\\nWe now exhibit a tiling that meets the lower bound.\\n\\n**Top tiles.**  For each $k\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ define the set of columns\\n\\\\[\\n    C_k:=\\\\{j\\\\mid r_j>k\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nIn other words, $C_k$ consists of the columns whose uncovered square lies strictly below row $k$.  The rectangle\\n\\\\[\\n    R_k:=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le k,\\\\; j\\\\in C_k\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nis a valid tile: it is anchored at the top of the board, it never contains an uncovered square (by definition all uncovered squares in the involved columns are below row $k$), and the $R_k$ for different $k$ are pairwise disjoint because the column sets $C_k$ are nested ($C_{k+1}\\\\subseteq C_k$) while the row intervals $[1,k]$ are nested as well.  The family $\\\\{R_k\\\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}$ covers every cell that lies above an uncovered square.\\n\\n**Bottom tiles.**  Symmetrically, for each $k\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ let\\n\\\\[\\n    D_k:=\\\\{j\\\\mid r_j\\\\le k\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nand define the rectangle\\n\\\\[\\n    S_k:=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid k+1\\\\le i\\\\le n,\\\\; j\\\\in D_k\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEach $S_k$ is anchored at the bottom of the board, contains no uncovered square (all uncovered squares in the involved columns are at rows $\\\\le k$), and the $S_k$ are pairwise disjoint.  The family $\\\\{S_k\\\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}$ covers every cell that lies below an uncovered square.\\n\\nTogether the $2(n-1)$ rectangles $R_1,\\\\dots,R_{n-1},S_1,\\\\dots,S_{n-1}$ cover all $n^2-n$ squares that must be tiled, they never overlap, and each row and column still contains exactly one uncovered square.  Hence a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\\n\\n#### 5.  Conclusion\\n\\nWe have proved a lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles and exhibited a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n    2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nFor the concrete board $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n    2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the answer to the original problem is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Answer:** 4048\\n\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\"\n}"
